<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#minie-rifle">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minié rifle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Minié rifles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minie rifle was a muzzle-loading rifled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> firing a conical bullet that expanded into the barrel's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rifling">rifling</a>, allowing accurate long-range fire. It was widely used by British and French forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>'s lack of such weapons exposed its military backwardness and encouraged later reforms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minie-rifle.jpg" alt="Minié rifle" width="320" height="94" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: military postcard of 1870's · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minie_rifle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The rifle took its name from the Minié ball, a bullet invented in 1849 by the French Army captain Claude-Étienne Minié of the Chasseurs d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and Henri-Gustave Delvigne. A version of the rifle was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for a short time in 1851, while the French Army adopted it officially only in 1857. Until then the smoothbore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a>, often loaded with buck and ball, had been the only practical field weapon. Rifled muskets had existed since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, but their bullets had to be hammered into the bore with a ramrod and mallet, and the short-lived carabine à tige system was hard to clean with the black powders of the period.</p><p>The bullet was a conical-cylindrical piece of soft lead, slightly smaller than the bore, with three grease-filled grooves and a conical hollow in its base. On firing, the expanding gas spread the skirted base into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rifling">rifling</a>, giving spin for accuracy, a better seal for longer range and a cleaner barrel. In a test at Vincennes in 1849 it passed through two boards of poplar at 15 yards. The large, easily deformed bullets, 14 to 18 mm across, caused terrible wounds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The British Army used the Pattern 1851 Minié rifle from 1851 to 1855; built to a Belgian design with the approval of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a>, 34,000 were made. At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> converted 300,000 older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> for Minié-type ammunition. Similar rifles firing Minié bullets, such as the Pattern 1853 Enfield, the Springfield Model 1861 and the Lorenz rifle, were the main infantry weapons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and the Chōshū and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> forces imported Minié rifles from 1862, and the fully equipped Chōshū troops won early victories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> (1868–1869).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>It is often said that the muzzle-loading Minié became obsolete in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> of 1864, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> armed with it were beaten by Prussians carrying the bolt-action Dreyse rifle, and again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>. Yet the Dreyse leaked gas and lost range, and the Bavarian army used the longer reach of its rifled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> well, so the question is debated. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> existing Minié rifles were converted to breech-loading Tabatière (snuff-box) rifles, and the Chassepot that soon followed, with greater range and rate of fire, arguably ended the type.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minié rifle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russian troops armed largely with smoothbore muskets faced Anglo-French infantry equipped with <b>Minié rifles</b>, and the empire could not supply its own Crimean peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
